<ContentPage title="Trading House" subtitle="Economic self-sufficiency through smart commerce.">
  <p>
    The Trading House is BARK's economic engine — connecting local production with regional and global markets.
    It handles procurement, sales, logistics, and financial operations for the community.
  </p>

  <h2>Functions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Export</strong> — Yungas coffee, cacao, tropical fruits, and value-added products to international markets via fair trade channels</li>
    <li><strong>Import</strong> — bulk procurement of materials and equipment the community needs but can't produce locally</li>
    <li><strong>Local trade</strong> — integration with Coroico markets, supporting local producers and buyers</li>
    <li><strong>Financial resilience</strong> — multi-currency operations, crypto capabilities, barter networks for crisis scenarios</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Value-Added Processing</h2>
  <p>
    Raw materials leave as finished products wherever possible. Coffee is roasted and branded. Cacao becomes chocolate.
    Fruit becomes preserves. Each step adds value and builds capability within the community.
  </p>

  <h2>Economic Integration</h2>
  <p>
    BARK doesn't compete with the local economy — it amplifies it. The Trading House creates channels that
    local producers can also use. Shared logistics, shared market access, shared learning.
    A rising tide approach.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-bark-sand rounded-xl border border-bark-green/10">
    <p class="text-bark-green font-semibold mb-2">Trade, logistics, agriculture expertise?</p>
    <p><a href="/en/participate">We need people who understand commerce</a>.</p>
  </div>
</ContentPage>
